    "frames": [
      {
        "slide": 1,
        "role": "cover",
        "subject": "a water cooled copper hearth seen at a low macro angle, with four rows of five pockets running away into a dark room. One arc melted alloy button glows gold to red in the middle pocket of the second row from the front. Cooled silver grey domes sit in the other pockets, and the near row and its outer pockets are cropped by the frame edges",
        "visual_consequence": "one hot button among cold ones on real copper. Its glow lights the copper around its pocket, and the far rows shrink into the dark. Without the headline a reader sees metal being made by heat, one melt at a time, on physical equipment and not on a screen. At 432 px the glow is the brightest point and the copper reads as metal",
        "weakness": "Accepted on these exact source and render hashes. Minor shortfalls that do not fail the audition: the lower right copper face is a smooth tan specular gradient without the planned milled arcs, and a straight, slightly sloped value step at design y about 605 where the navy room meets the near black far bench reads as scene geometry.",
