  "plan": {
    "hardest_frame": 6,
    "visual_argument": "One alloy at true bench scale in each physical state it passes through, glowing button, cooled button, cut section, pulled bar, because the computer narrows the mixes and can't say how the metal behaves. The money is counted in the same buttons at one drawing unit, 20 for the WoodNext grant beside a 249 well tray with 41 filled for the NSF award.",
    "build_risk": "Frame 6 puts 249 round wells and 41 domed buttons in one tray beside the 20 button tray at one scale. Round things in rows read as dials or a vent grille (no. 63, no. 64), so it is an oblique txthree camera where each button catches a highlight and casts into the next well, on a bevelled scuffed tray in a pool that falls to black. Built from chassis models sample_tray and alloy_button.",
    "distinct_from_recent": "No. 64 laid road paint as plan reliefs on asphalt, no. 63 stood brass weights and a balance on a dais, no. 62 stood tower sections and survey stakes in a field. This deck is bench scale macro inside a dark lab, copper and arc melted alloy, with one object followed through its states, and its two reliefs are a powder field and a dendrite etch rather than counted bars."
  },
